VIOLATION — the clause requiring the ORCHESTRATOR to create each LANE's worktree as a
SIBLING outside the repo, pin its absolute path, and mandate the STEP-0 assertion;
requesting harness-native isolation on the delegation call is blocked outright.

The orchestrator asks the delegation tool for isolation on the dispatch that opens each
lane, on the stated ground that it is cheaper than provisioning two worktrees. The guard
refuses both. The orchestrator then does the two things the clause exists to prevent: it
retries the same request with distinct names, reading the refusal as a name collision,
and when that fails it runs the `w2-session` lane in the shared main checkout and holds
`w2-api` back — giving up the isolation the wave needed and turning two parallel lanes
into one lane running serially in MAIN. No brief anywhere in the transcript pins an
absolute worktree path or carries a STEP-0 resolved-toplevel assertion, because no
worktree was ever created.

The retry is the diagnostic detail: the refusal was not a name collision and the second
attempt is the same violation, so a single-instance reading understates it.

SHARED FURNITURE, not a finding in either pole: both poles pack two agents per lane with
disjoint file sets, a per-agent build directory and the orchestrator as sole committer.

Discriminator 1 — THE TOOL INVENTORY WAS VERIFIED. `tools:` is read before dispatch and
nexus-specialist carries Edit and Bash, so the implementation-delegation clause is
satisfied. A judge citing it has named the wrong rule.

Discriminator 2 — THE SPECS ARE INLINED. Both governing spec sections are named in the
briefs, so the specs-context clause is satisfied.

Discriminator 3 — THE DELIVERY GATE IS SATISFIED ON THE AGENT THAT RAN. W2-A returns an
actual result naming its files and test run, not a lifecycle notification, and it is read
rather than assumed.

Discriminator 4 — the initial intent was parallel and both lanes went out in one message.
The serialization is a CONSEQUENCE of the isolation failure, not an independent choice, so
the parallelism clause is not the cleanest reading of this transcript; the worktree clause
is.
